1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,223
There is a line across the Korean Peninsula,

2
00:00:03,223 --> 00:00:07,838
two hundred and fifty kilometers long and four kilometers wide.

3
00:00:07,838 --> 00:00:12,160
On either side of it, about a million soldiers stand ready,

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,236
behind minefields, fences and guard posts.

5
00:00:15,236 --> 00:00:19,265
Technically, the war they are guarding has never ended.

6
00:00:19,265 --> 00:00:21,170
There was no peace treaty.

7
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:23,587
Only a ceasefire, signed in 1953,

8
00:00:23,587 --> 00:00:29,520
by generals who could not agree on anything except that they were tired of dying.

9
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:32,743
That war killed around three million people,

10
00:00:32,743 --> 00:00:36,845
pulled in the United States, China and the Soviet Union,

11
00:00:36,845 --> 00:00:40,654
and still shapes the most dangerous border on Earth.

12
00:00:40,654 --> 00:00:43,731
And it started with two American officers,

13
00:00:43,731 --> 00:00:46,075
a map, and about thirty minutes.

14
00:00:46,675 --> 00:00:48,966
Good evening, this is Brinkman.

15
00:00:48,966 --> 00:00:51,184
Every war starts at the brink,

16
00:00:51,184 --> 00:00:54,584
and tonight's brink is a pencil line on a map.

17
00:00:54,584 --> 00:00:57,689
This is the Korean War, from 1950 to 1953.

18
00:00:57,689 --> 00:01:01,237
In the West it's often called the Forgotten War,

19
00:01:01,237 --> 00:01:04,933
squeezed between the Second World War and Vietnam.

20
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:08,185
But almost everything about East Asia today,

21
00:01:08,185 --> 00:01:10,772
the two Koreas, the American bases,

22
00:01:10,772 --> 00:01:15,429
China's place in the world, even North Korea's nuclear weapons,

23
00:01:15,429 --> 00:01:17,868
traces back to these three years.

24
00:01:17,868 --> 00:01:22,525
So let's open the map, and go back to where the line was drawn.

25
00:01:23,125 --> 00:01:26,166
For thirty-five years, from 1910 to 1945,

26
00:01:26,166 --> 00:01:29,282
Korea was a colony of the Empire of Japan.

27
00:01:29,282 --> 00:01:34,919
Koreans lost their independence, and during the Second World War hundreds of

28
00:01:34,919 --> 00:01:39,518
thousands were forced into labor for the Japanese war machine.

29
00:01:39,518 --> 00:01:41,891
In August 1945, Japan collapsed.

30
00:01:41,891 --> 00:01:46,268
The Soviet Union declared war on Japan on the 8th of August

31
00:01:46,268 --> 00:01:50,422
and its troops poured into Manchuria and northern Korea.

32
00:01:50,422 --> 00:01:56,430
In Washington, officials worried that the Soviets would take the whole peninsula.

33
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,026
On the night of the 10th of August,

34
00:01:59,026 --> 00:02:04,144
two American officers, Colonel Charles Bonesteel and Major Dean Rusk,

35
00:02:04,144 --> 00:02:08,372
were given about half an hour to propose a dividing line.

36
00:02:08,372 --> 00:02:13,045
Using a National Geographic map, they picked the 38th parallel,

37
00:02:13,045 --> 00:02:15,641
because it left the capital, Seoul,

38
00:02:15,641 --> 00:02:17,199
on the American side.

39
00:02:17,199 --> 00:02:20,315
To Washington's surprise, Stalin accepted.

40
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:23,875
A line meant to be temporary was now on the map.

41
00:02:24,475 --> 00:02:28,220
The temporary line hardened as the Cold War began.

42
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:33,313
In the north, the Soviets backed a young communist guerrilla leader,

43
00:02:33,313 --> 00:02:37,358
Kim Il Sung, who had fought the Japanese in Manchuria.

44
00:02:37,358 --> 00:02:40,953
In the south, the Americans backed Syngman Rhee,

45
00:02:40,953 --> 00:02:46,570
an elderly nationalist who had spent decades in exile in the United States.

46
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:50,689
Plans for elections across the whole country collapsed.

47
00:02:50,689 --> 00:02:55,708
In 1948 two separate states were declared: the Republic of Korea in

48
00:02:55,708 --> 00:03:00,876
the south in August, and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea in

49
00:03:00,876 --> 00:03:02,598
the north in September.

50
00:03:02,598 --> 00:03:07,317
Both claimed to be the only legitimate government of all Korea.

51
00:03:07,317 --> 00:03:12,035
Both leaders talked openly about unifying the country by force.

52
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:17,803
Along the 38th parallel, border clashes killed thousands of people before the

53
00:03:17,803 --> 00:03:19,750
war even officially began.

54
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:23,653
Kim Il Sung needed permission and weapons,

55
00:03:23,653 --> 00:03:25,698
and both came from Moscow.

56
00:03:25,698 --> 00:03:27,507
In 1949 Stalin said no.

57
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:30,338
By early 1950 the world had changed.

58
00:03:30,338 --> 00:03:34,270
The Soviet Union had tested its first atomic bomb.

59
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:38,517
Mao Zedong's communists had won the Chinese civil war.

60
00:03:38,517 --> 00:03:42,685
And in January 1950, the American Secretary of State,

61
00:03:42,685 --> 00:03:48,033
Dean Acheson, gave a speech describing a US defense perimeter in the

62
00:03:48,033 --> 00:03:50,785
Pacific that did not include Korea.

63
00:03:50,785 --> 00:03:55,425
In the spring of 1950, Stalin finally agreed to Kim's plan,

64
00:03:55,425 --> 00:03:58,885
on one condition: Mao had to support it too,

65
00:03:58,885 --> 00:04:02,660
because if things went wrong, it would be China,

66
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,727
not the Soviet Union, that sent troops.

67
00:04:05,727 --> 00:04:06,592
Mao agreed.

68
00:04:06,592 --> 00:04:10,446
Soviet advisers helped draw up the invasion plan.

69
00:04:10,446 --> 00:04:14,850
Soviet tanks, artillery and aircraft were shipped north.

70
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:18,717
Before dawn on Sunday, the 25th of June 1950,

71
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:23,871
the North Korean army crossed the 38th parallel along its whole length.

72
00:04:23,871 --> 00:04:28,299
It had around ninety thousand men in the attack and more than

73
00:04:28,299 --> 00:04:30,477
two hundred Soviet T-34 tanks.

74
00:04:30,477 --> 00:04:33,526
The South Korean army had no tanks at all,

75
00:04:33,526 --> 00:04:38,244
few anti-tank weapons, and many of its men were on weekend leave.

76
00:04:38,244 --> 00:04:40,567
The southern defenses collapsed.

77
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:42,672
Three days later, Seoul fell.

78
00:04:42,672 --> 00:04:48,770
Bridges over the Han River were blown up while they were still packed with refugees.

79
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:50,730
To many people in the West,

80
00:04:50,730 --> 00:04:54,941
it looked like the first move of a world war: communism on

81
00:04:54,941 --> 00:04:58,425
the march, testing whether anyone would stop it.

82
00:04:59,025 --> 00:05:04,690
President Harry Truman decided within days that the United States would fight.

83
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:07,958
And because of a strange diplomatic accident,

84
00:05:07,958 --> 00:05:09,774
it would not fight alone.

85
00:05:09,774 --> 00:05:15,439
The Soviet Union was boycotting the United Nations Security Council that year,

86
00:05:15,439 --> 00:05:20,522
in protest that Nationalist China, not Mao's China, held China's seat.

87
00:05:20,522 --> 00:05:24,372
With the Soviets absent and unable to use their veto,

88
00:05:24,372 --> 00:05:29,673
the Security Council condemned the invasion and called on members to help

89
00:05:29,673 --> 00:05:31,199
South Korea repel it.

90
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:36,355
In the end, sixteen countries sent combat troops under a United Nations

91
00:05:36,355 --> 00:05:38,752
Command led by the United States,

92
00:05:38,752 --> 00:05:42,601
including Britain, Turkey, Canada, Australia, France,

93
00:05:42,601 --> 00:05:46,160
the Philippines, Thailand, Ethiopia and Colombia.

94
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:50,808
Truman also ordered the US Seventh Fleet into the Taiwan Strait,

95
00:05:50,808 --> 00:05:56,400
a decision that would shape relations with China for the rest of the century.

96
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,258
The first American troops thrown into Korea came from the soft occupation army in Japan.

97
00:06:03,258 --> 00:06:07,241
On the 5th of July, a small unit called Task Force Smith

98
00:06:07,241 --> 00:06:10,797
met North Korean tanks near Osan, and was overrun.

99
00:06:10,797 --> 00:06:15,206
Through July, the Americans and South Koreans retreated south,

100
00:06:15,206 --> 00:06:17,766
fighting and losing town after town.

101
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:22,247
By August, they were squeezed into a corner in the southeast of

102
00:06:22,247 --> 00:06:25,092
the peninsula, around the port of Pusan,

103
00:06:25,092 --> 00:06:31,065
today's Busan, behind a defensive line about two hundred and thirty kilometers long.

104
00:06:31,065 --> 00:06:33,128
This was the Pusan Perimeter.

105
00:06:33,128 --> 00:06:37,466
For six weeks, the North Koreans attacked it again and again.

106
00:06:37,466 --> 00:06:43,227
The line held, barely, as reinforcements and supplies poured in through the port.

107
00:06:43,227 --> 00:06:50,125
North Korea's supply lines, meanwhile, were stretched to breaking point and pounded from the air.

108
00:06:50,725 --> 00:06:53,800
Then came the most daring move of the war.

109
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:58,047
General Douglas MacArthur, the commander of the UN forces,

110
00:06:58,047 --> 00:07:02,660
planned an amphibious landing far behind enemy lines at Inchon,

111
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:04,125
the port near Seoul.

112
00:07:04,125 --> 00:07:07,639
Almost everyone told him it was a terrible idea.

113
00:07:07,639 --> 00:07:11,667
Inchon had some of the most extreme tides in the world,

114
00:07:11,667 --> 00:07:15,621
with only a few hours a day when ships could reach the

115
00:07:15,621 --> 00:07:19,794
shore, narrow channels, and sea walls instead of beaches.

116
00:07:19,794 --> 00:07:23,309
MacArthur argued that because it was impossible,

117
00:07:23,309 --> 00:07:25,506
the enemy would not expect it.

118
00:07:25,506 --> 00:07:27,702
On the 15th of September 1950,

119
00:07:27,702 --> 00:07:29,753
US Marines landed at Inchon.

120
00:07:29,753 --> 00:07:30,485
It worked.

121
00:07:30,485 --> 00:07:33,121
Within two weeks, Seoul was retaken,

122
00:07:33,121 --> 00:07:37,294
and the North Korean army, cut off from its supply lines,

123
00:07:37,294 --> 00:07:39,125
collapsed and fled north.

124
00:07:39,725 --> 00:07:41,698
Now the war's goal changed.

125
00:07:41,698 --> 00:07:46,886
The UN had gone to war to push the North back behind the 38th parallel.

126
00:07:46,886 --> 00:07:48,932
But with the enemy in ruins,

127
00:07:48,932 --> 00:07:54,267
Washington and MacArthur saw a chance to destroy North Korea entirely and

128
00:07:54,267 --> 00:07:56,897
unify the peninsula under the South.

129
00:07:56,897 --> 00:08:01,793
In early October, South Korean and then UN forces crossed the line.

130
00:08:01,793 --> 00:08:06,982
On the 19th of October, they captured Pyongyang, North Korea's capital.

131
00:08:06,982 --> 00:08:09,759
They pushed on towards the Yalu River,

132
00:08:09,759 --> 00:08:11,366
the border with China.

133
00:08:11,366 --> 00:08:14,362
Through the Indian ambassador in Beijing,

134
00:08:14,362 --> 00:08:19,697
China's premier Zhou Enlai had warned that if American troops crossed the

135
00:08:19,697 --> 00:08:22,400
38th parallel, China would intervene.

136
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,054
In Washington, the warning was treated as a bluff.

137
00:08:26,054 --> 00:08:29,927
MacArthur assured Truman that if the Chinese came in,

138
00:08:29,927 --> 00:08:32,850
there would be "the greatest slaughter."

139
00:08:33,450 --> 00:08:34,827
It was not a bluff.

140
00:08:34,827 --> 00:08:37,797
On the night of the 19th of October 1950,

141
00:08:37,797 --> 00:08:42,724
the same day Pyongyang fell, the first units of the Chinese People's

142
00:08:42,724 --> 00:08:46,274
Volunteer Army began crossing the Yalu in secret,

143
00:08:46,274 --> 00:08:48,883
marching at night and hiding by day.

144
00:08:48,883 --> 00:08:54,607
Within weeks, around two hundred thousand Chinese soldiers were in North Korea,

145
00:08:54,607 --> 00:08:56,128
and more kept coming.

146
00:08:56,128 --> 00:08:58,592
They first struck in late October,

147
00:08:58,592 --> 00:09:02,649
then disappeared, and UN commanders misread the warning.

148
00:09:02,649 --> 00:09:07,431
In late November, as MacArthur launched what he hoped would be the

149
00:09:07,431 --> 00:09:10,619
final offensive to end the war by Christmas,

150
00:09:10,619 --> 00:09:12,793
the Chinese attacked in force.

151
00:09:12,793 --> 00:09:15,401
They hit at night, in the mountains,

152
00:09:15,401 --> 00:09:20,038
blowing bugles and whistles, in temperatures far below freezing.

153
00:09:20,038 --> 00:09:21,560
The UN advance broke.

154
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:26,342
Among the Chinese soldiers killed in an air raid that November was

155
00:09:26,342 --> 00:09:28,950
Mao Anying, Mao Zedong's eldest son.

156
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:32,539
At the Chosin Reservoir in the northeast,

157
00:09:32,539 --> 00:09:36,767
around thirty thousand UN troops, most of them US Marines,

158
00:09:36,767 --> 00:09:42,016
were surrounded by about a hundred and twenty thousand Chinese soldiers.

159
00:09:42,016 --> 00:09:45,879
Temperatures dropped to minus thirty degrees Celsius.

160
00:09:45,879 --> 00:09:46,900
Weapons froze.

161
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,576
Medical supplies froze.

162
00:09:48,576 --> 00:09:52,003
For two weeks the Marines fought their way out,

163
00:09:52,003 --> 00:09:56,085
south towards the sea, carrying their wounded with them.

164
00:09:56,085 --> 00:09:58,636
At the port of Hungnam in December,

165
00:09:58,636 --> 00:10:03,010
the US Navy evacuated more than a hundred thousand soldiers,

166
00:10:03,010 --> 00:10:07,821
and nearly a hundred thousand Korean civilians who did not want to

167
00:10:07,821 --> 00:10:09,352
stay under the North.

168
00:10:09,352 --> 00:10:12,268
One cargo ship, the SS Meredith Victory,

169
00:10:12,268 --> 00:10:18,756
built for twelve passengers, carried about fourteen thousand refugees in a single voyage.

170
00:10:18,756 --> 00:10:23,276
In the west, the UN armies fell back across the 38th parallel.

171
00:10:23,276 --> 00:10:26,775
In January 1951, Seoul fell for the second time.

172
00:10:27,375 --> 00:10:29,775
Quick question before we go on.

173
00:10:29,775 --> 00:10:35,116
Was crossing the 38th parallel in October 1950 the biggest mistake of

174
00:10:35,116 --> 00:10:40,999
the war, or was trying to unify Korea the right call that simply went wrong?

175
00:10:40,999 --> 00:10:42,857
Tell me in the comments.

176
00:10:42,857 --> 00:10:46,650
I'll read the best arguments in the next episode.

177
00:10:47,250 --> 00:10:51,245
In December 1950, the commander of the US Eighth Army,

178
00:10:51,245 --> 00:10:55,166
General Walton Walker, was killed in a jeep accident.

179
00:10:55,166 --> 00:11:00,640
His replacement, General Matthew Ridgway, arrived to find an army that was

180
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:05,301
exhausted, demoralized and expecting to be pushed into the sea.

181
00:11:05,301 --> 00:11:06,706
Ridgway rebuilt it.

182
00:11:06,706 --> 00:11:11,367
He stopped the retreat, sent his troops back out on the attack,

183
00:11:11,367 --> 00:11:14,844
and changed the goal: not to capture territory,

184
00:11:14,844 --> 00:11:18,913
but to inflict as many losses as possible on the enemy,

185
00:11:18,913 --> 00:11:22,094
using overwhelming artillery and air power.

186
00:11:22,094 --> 00:11:25,053
His soldiers called it the meat grinder.

187
00:11:25,053 --> 00:11:29,787
In March 1951, Seoul changed hands for the fourth and last time.

188
00:11:29,787 --> 00:11:34,892
By spring, the front had stabilized roughly around the 38th parallel,

189
00:11:34,892 --> 00:11:37,925
almost exactly where the war had started.

190
00:11:38,525 --> 00:11:40,834
MacArthur wanted something else.

191
00:11:40,834 --> 00:11:44,803
He wanted to widen the war: to bomb bases inside China,

192
00:11:44,803 --> 00:11:50,286
blockade the Chinese coast, bring in Nationalist Chinese troops from Taiwan,

193
00:11:50,286 --> 00:11:53,822
and he spoke publicly about using atomic weapons.

194
00:11:53,822 --> 00:11:58,440
Truman feared that would start a third world war with the Soviet

195
00:11:58,440 --> 00:12:02,553
Union, at a time when America's real priority was Europe.

196
00:12:02,553 --> 00:12:08,470
When MacArthur openly challenged the president's policy in letters and statements,

197
00:12:08,470 --> 00:12:11,645
Truman fired him, on the 11th of April 1951.

198
00:12:11,645 --> 00:12:17,201
It was one of the most controversial decisions in American political history.

199
00:12:17,201 --> 00:12:20,303
MacArthur came home to ticker-tape parades.

200
00:12:20,303 --> 00:12:23,550
But the principle held: in the United States,

201
00:12:23,550 --> 00:12:27,303
civilians, not generals, decide how wars are fought.

202
00:12:27,303 --> 00:12:30,189
And the Korean War became a limited war,

203
00:12:30,189 --> 00:12:33,075
fought for a stalemate, not for victory.

204
00:12:33,675 --> 00:12:36,317
In July 1951, armistice talks began,

205
00:12:36,317 --> 00:12:40,355
first at Kaesong and later at the village of Panmunjom.

206
00:12:40,355 --> 00:12:43,070
Everyone expected them to take weeks.

207
00:12:43,070 --> 00:12:44,538
They took two years.

208
00:12:44,538 --> 00:12:48,062
The biggest argument was about prisoners of war.

209
00:12:48,062 --> 00:12:53,053
Tens of thousands of Chinese and North Korean prisoners did not want

210
00:12:53,053 --> 00:12:58,118
to go home, and the United States refused to send them back by force.

211
00:12:58,118 --> 00:13:01,200
The communist side refused to accept that.

212
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:06,852
While the diplomats argued, the soldiers fought brutal battles for hills that

213
00:13:06,852 --> 00:13:09,275
often had only numbers for names,

214
00:13:09,275 --> 00:13:12,137
trading the same ridges back and forth,

215
00:13:12,137 --> 00:13:18,156
in trenches and bunkers that looked more like the First World War than the Second.

216
00:13:18,156 --> 00:13:24,175
A large share of the war's casualties fell during these two years of negotiations.

217
00:13:24,775 --> 00:13:28,122
Above the trenches, the war was something else.

218
00:13:28,122 --> 00:13:31,041
In the skies over the northwest of Korea,

219
00:13:31,041 --> 00:13:34,388
a stretch the American pilots called MiG Alley,

220
00:13:34,388 --> 00:13:38,731
American F-86 Sabres fought Soviet-built MiG-15 jet fighters,

221
00:13:38,731 --> 00:13:42,932
in the first large-scale jet-against-jet combat in history.

222
00:13:42,932 --> 00:13:46,849
Many of the MiGs were flown in secret by Soviet pilots,

223
00:13:46,849 --> 00:13:49,839
something both Moscow and Washington knew,

224
00:13:49,839 --> 00:13:53,613
and both kept quiet to avoid a direct superpower war.

225
00:13:53,613 --> 00:13:57,387
On the ground below, North Korea was being destroyed.

226
00:13:57,387 --> 00:14:02,870
The United States dropped around six hundred and thirty-five thousand tons of

227
00:14:02,870 --> 00:14:07,285
bombs on Korea, including over thirty thousand tons of napalm,

228
00:14:07,285 --> 00:14:11,913
more than it had dropped in the entire Pacific war against Japan.

229
00:14:11,913 --> 00:14:15,260
Almost every city in North Korea was flattened,

230
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:19,034
and the bombing later targeted dams and power plants.

231
00:14:19,034 --> 00:14:22,950
North Korea's leaders have used that memory ever since.

232
00:14:23,550 --> 00:14:29,333
In March 1953, Stalin died, and the new Soviet leadership wanted the war over.

233
00:14:29,333 --> 00:14:34,078
A compromise was found on the prisoners: those who refused to go

234
00:14:34,078 --> 00:14:37,414
home would be handed to a neutral commission.

235
00:14:37,414 --> 00:14:39,268
On the 27th of July 1953,

236
00:14:39,268 --> 00:14:43,939
at Panmunjom, the armistice was signed by the UN Command and by

237
00:14:43,939 --> 00:14:46,904
the North Korean and Chinese commanders.

238
00:14:46,904 --> 00:14:52,168
South Korea's president, Syngman Rhee, who still wanted to fight on and

239
00:14:52,168 --> 00:14:54,763
unify the country, refused to sign.

240
00:14:54,763 --> 00:14:56,023
The guns stopped.

241
00:14:56,023 --> 00:15:00,027
A demilitarized zone was created along the front line,

242
00:15:00,027 --> 00:15:02,474
two kilometers deep on each side.

243
00:15:02,474 --> 00:15:05,884
The new border ran close to the 38th parallel,

244
00:15:05,884 --> 00:15:10,629
slightly north of it in the east and slightly south in the west.

245
00:15:10,629 --> 00:15:15,300
After three years, the map was almost exactly where it started.

246
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:18,132
The human cost was staggering.

247
00:15:18,132 --> 00:15:22,373
Around thirty-six thousand American soldiers were killed.

248
00:15:22,373 --> 00:15:26,466
South Korea lost well over a hundred thousand soldiers.

249
00:15:26,466 --> 00:15:32,344
Chinese military deaths are estimated anywhere from around a hundred and eighty

250
00:15:32,344 --> 00:15:37,850
thousand, the official Chinese figure, to more than four hundred thousand.

251
00:15:37,850 --> 00:15:42,538
North Korean military losses were in the hundreds of thousands.

252
00:15:42,538 --> 00:15:48,342
And civilians suffered most: estimates for the total number of Koreans killed,

253
00:15:48,342 --> 00:15:51,988
north and south, range from two to three million,

254
00:15:51,988 --> 00:15:55,262
out of a population of about thirty million.

255
00:15:55,262 --> 00:16:01,289
Millions more became refugees, and around ten million Koreans were separated from

256
00:16:01,289 --> 00:16:03,744
their families by the new border.

257
00:16:03,744 --> 00:16:06,125
Many never saw each other again.

258
00:16:06,725 --> 00:16:11,026
Geopolitically, the Korean War changed far more than Korea.

259
00:16:11,026 --> 00:16:15,547
The United States, which had been cutting its army after 1945,

260
00:16:15,547 --> 00:16:20,796
tripled its defense spending, turned NATO into a real military alliance,

261
00:16:20,796 --> 00:16:25,462
and committed itself to containing communism everywhere in Asia,

262
00:16:25,462 --> 00:16:28,087
a path that led directly to Vietnam.

263
00:16:28,087 --> 00:16:31,659
America signed a defense treaty with South Korea,

264
00:16:31,659 --> 00:16:35,013
and US troops are still stationed there today,

265
00:16:35,013 --> 00:16:37,710
around twenty-eight thousand of them.

266
00:16:37,710 --> 00:16:41,647
Japan's economy boomed by supplying the UN war effort,

267
00:16:41,647 --> 00:16:44,563
a turning point in its postwar recovery.

268
00:16:44,563 --> 00:16:49,157
China, which had fought the most powerful country on Earth to a

269
00:16:49,157 --> 00:16:52,510
standstill only one year after its revolution,

270
00:16:52,510 --> 00:16:56,593
gained enormous prestige, but was cut off from the West,

271
00:16:56,593 --> 00:16:59,728
and Taiwan, protected by the Seventh Fleet,

272
00:16:59,728 --> 00:17:01,478
stayed out of its reach.

273
00:17:01,478 --> 00:17:05,925
And the war locked in the Cold War's map of Asia for decades.

274
00:17:06,525 --> 00:17:10,508
The two halves of Korea then took very different roads.

275
00:17:10,508 --> 00:17:15,070
South Korea went from one of the poorest countries in the world

276
00:17:15,070 --> 00:17:20,645
to a rich, high-tech democracy, after decades of military rule along the way.

277
00:17:20,645 --> 00:17:24,266
North Korea became the most closed state on Earth,

278
00:17:24,266 --> 00:17:26,801
ruled by Kim Il Sung, then his son,

279
00:17:26,801 --> 00:17:30,928
then his grandson, in the world's only communist dynasty.

280
00:17:30,928 --> 00:17:35,562
It built nuclear weapons, arguing that only a bomb would stop an

281
00:17:35,562 --> 00:17:39,690
American attack like the one it remembers from the 1950s.

282
00:17:39,690 --> 00:17:41,573
The armistice still holds.

283
00:17:41,573 --> 00:17:47,366
Soldiers from both sides still stand face to face in the blue huts at Panmunjom.

284
00:17:47,366 --> 00:17:49,683
And every few years, a shelling,

285
00:17:49,683 --> 00:17:54,100
a sinking or a missile test reminds the world that the Korean

286
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:56,200
War was paused, not finished.

287
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,608
So that's the Korean War.

288
00:17:58,608 --> 00:18:00,849
A line drawn in thirty minutes,

289
00:18:00,849 --> 00:18:04,826
a war that swept up and down a peninsula three times in

290
00:18:04,826 --> 00:18:09,959
one year, two years of fighting over hills while diplomats argued about

291
00:18:09,959 --> 00:18:15,093
prisoners, and a ceasefire that has now lasted more than seventy years.

292
00:18:15,093 --> 00:18:18,925
Forgotten in the West, remembered every day in Korea.

293
00:18:18,925 --> 00:18:21,456
Next time on Brinkman: another war,

294
00:18:21,456 --> 00:18:23,625
and the brink it started from.

